{
  "concept": "Merge sort works bottom-up as one repeated operation — merging two already-sorted runs into one longer sorted run — applied at every level from singletons up to the final sequence.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "top label 'sorted sequence'",
    "top wide box, 8 cells reading 1 2 2 3 4 5 6 7",
    "two level-2 boxes reading 2 4 5 7 (left) and 1 2 3 6 (right)",
    "four level-1 boxes reading 2 5, 4 7, 1 3, 2 6 (left to right)",
    "eight singleton boxes reading 5, 2, 4, 7, 1, 3, 2, 6 (left to right)",
    "bottom label 'initial sequence'",
    "thick gray arrow pairs linking adjacent child boxes to their parent box at each level",
    "'merge' captions beside each arrow pair (three levels)",
    "cell divider lines in every multi-element box",
    "subscripts tagging the duplicate 2 values (visible when the stability trace is on)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "mergeImplementation",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "label": "Implement merge(left, right)",
      "entry_point": "merge(left, right)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "front(side)", "does": "read the front (first not-yet-taken) value of run 'left' or 'right'; undefined when that run is empty" },
        { "op": "isEmpty(side)", "does": "true once every value in that run has been taken" },
        { "op": "lessOrEqual(a, b)", "does": "compare two values — choosing <= over < is exactly what decides what happens on ties" },
        { "op": "take(side)", "does": "append the front value of that run to the output box — the ONLY way to build the sorted result" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "drop a text marker on the current frame, shown in the replay trace" }
      ],
      "frame": "the two active runs and the partially filled output box, with a pointer on each run's front element and the next output slot highlighted",
      "input": { "label": "Runs to merge (left | right)", "default": "2, 4, 5, 7 | 1, 2, 3, 6" },
      "sample_code": "while (!isEmpty(\"left\") && !isEmpty(\"right\")) {\n  if (lessOrEqual(front(\"left\"), front(\"right\"))) {\n    take(\"left\");      // ties go to the LEFT run\n  } else {\n    take(\"right\");\n  }\n}\nwhile (!isEmpty(\"left\")) take(\"left\");   // drain leftovers\nwhile (!isEmpty(\"right\")) take(\"right\");",
      "buggy_code": "while (!isEmpty(\"left\") && !isEmpty(\"right\")) {\n  if (front(\"left\") < front(\"right\")) {   // BUG: strict < sends ties to the right run, destroying stability\n    take(\"left\");\n  } else {\n    take(\"right\");\n  }\n}\nwhile (!isEmpty(\"left\")) take(\"left\");\nwhile (!isEmpty(\"right\")) take(\"right\");",
      "success_check": "the output contains all values in non-decreasing order, AND for equal front values the left run's copy is taken before the right run's copy (the merge is stable)",
      "teaches": "Merging two sorted runs is a front-pointer procedure: compare the two fronts, take the smaller (left copy on ties), and repeat — the replay shows exactly in what order values enter the output and why"
    },
    {
      "id": "mergeLevel",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Active merge tier",
      "states": ["level 1: singletons → pairs", "level 2: pairs → quads", "level 3: quads → final"],
      "default": "level 1: singletons → pairs",
      "teaches": "Every level of the tree does the same merge work on runs twice as long and half as many — the doubling of run length versus halving of run count is the source of the n log n running time"
    },
    {
      "id": "stabilityTrace",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Stability trace",
      "options": ["Untagged values", "Tag duplicate 2s"],
      "default": "Untagged values",
      "teaches": "Tagging the two duplicate 2s shows merge never inverts their relative order — taking the left run's copy on ties is what makes merge sort stable, not an accident of the data"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Read the lattice bottom-up",
      "narration": "Eight singletons merge into four sorted pairs, then two sorted quads, then one sorted sequence. Box width scales with run length, so each tier doubles the sorted-run size and halves the run count.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "level 1: singletons → pairs", "stabilityTrace": "Untagged values" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Write the merge routine",
      "narration": "The active pair and destination highlight at the final tier. Implement merge(left, right) with the front/isEmpty/take primitives and watch the replay fill the output box one comparison at a time — this one routine is the whole algorithm.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "level 3: quads → final", "stabilityTrace": "Untagged values" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Prove stability on the 2s",
      "narration": "Turn on the stability trace and rerun: the two 2s stay in their original left-then-right order only if ties resolve toward the left run. Swap <= for < and the trace shows the order flip while the values still look sorted.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "level 3: quads → final", "stabilityTrace": "Tag duplicate 2s" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Merge sort does exactly one thing — merge two already-sorted runs, front by front — and applies it at log n levels where total work per level is n; the only subtlety is that ties must favor the left run to keep the sort stable.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Serif textbook styling on white. Five horizontal ranks: eight single-cell gray boxes at the bottom under the label 'initial sequence'; four two-cell gray boxes above; two four-cell gray boxes above those; one eight-cell gray box at top under the label 'sorted sequence'. Box width scales with the number of cells so doubling reads visually. Cells are light gray with thin black borders and black dividers; values centered in each cell. Thick gray arrows rise from each pair of child boxes up to their parent box, with the italic word 'merge' between each arrow pair. Highlight the active tier by dimming all other ranks. When the stability trace is on, mark the two cells containing 2 (one in the third singleton/second quad box, one in the fourth singleton... i.e. the duplicate 2s in the left and right runs) with small colored subscript tags 2-and-2 that persist through merge levels so their relative order stays observable."
}

